course: french
lesson: 34
title: France and the French-speaking world
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: French is not only the language of France: it is spoken on every continent, and all the people and places that speak it together are called la francophonie.
explain: In fact more French speakers live in Africa than in Europe, in big cities such as Kinshasa, Abidjan and Dakar, and many of them speak an African language at home as well.
explain: Picture a video call with four penpals: one in Brussels says septante for 70, one in Montreal says "Mets ta tuque !" for "Put your woolly hat on!", one in Dakar mixes French and Wolof, and one in Paris says it all the way your textbook does.
explain: Each place has its own words and habits, just as English sounds different in Glasgow, Texas and Sydney - none of them is wrong.
explain: France itself reaches far beyond Europe: Martinique and Guadeloupe in the Caribbean, La Réunion in the Indian Ocean and French Guiana in South America are all part of France.
fact: La francophonie means all the people and countries that speak French; French is spoken on every continent.
fact: More French speakers live in Africa than in Europe; big French-speaking cities include Paris, Kinshasa, Abidjan, Montreal and Dakar.
fact: Belgium has three official languages, French, Dutch and German; French-speaking Belgians say septante (70) and nonante (90), and Bruxelles is said "bru-SEL".
fact: Switzerland has four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh; French is spoken in the west, around Geneva (Genève) and Lausanne.
fact: In Quebec, Bonjour ! can also be said when leaving a shop, a bit like "have a good day"; in France you say au revoir.
fact: Quebec has its own words: une tuque is a woolly hat (un bonnet in France) and un dépanneur is a corner shop.
fact: Meal names differ: in France le petit-déjeuner is breakfast, le déjeuner is lunch and le dîner is the evening meal; in Quebec le dîner is lunch and le souper is the evening meal.
fact: In Senegal many people speak French and Wolof; in many West African countries people speak French and one or more African languages.
fact: In Côte d'Ivoire, "On dit quoi ?" is a friendly "What's up?", and "Ça va un peu" means "I'm okay, so-so".
fact: Martinique, Guadeloupe, La Réunion and French Guiana (la Guyane) are part of France; people there use the euro and often speak a Creole as well as French.
fact: Jersey and Guernsey, the Channel Islands, are close to France and have French street names (rue means street), but they are not part of France.
fact: New Caledonia (la Nouvelle-Calédonie) is a French territory in the Pacific, not far from Australia and New Zealand, with its capital Nouméa, where people speak French and Kanak languages.
fact: Paris, the capital of France, is said "pa-REE" and stands on the river Seine; the Eiffel Tower, la tour Eiffel, was built for a world fair in 1889.
exercise: What is la francophonie? | All the places that speak French | The French national football team | A French radio station for children | The part of Paris where the king lived
exercise: Where do more French speakers live? | In Africa | In Europe | In South America | In Asia
exercise: How do French-speaking Belgians say 70? | septante | soixante-dix | huitante | septième
exercise: How do you say Bruxelles? | "bru-SEL" | "bruk-SELLS" | "brux-ELL-ess" | "BRUKS-uls"
exercise: Harvey sees French signs in Geneva. Why? | Western Switzerland speaks French | Geneva is a city in France | The signs were put up for tourists | All of Switzerland speaks only French
exercise: How many national languages does Switzerland have? | Four | One | Two | Three
exercise: Grace's cousin in Montreal says "Bonjour !" when leaving a shop. What is going on? | In Quebec it can also mean goodbye | She forgot the word for goodbye | She wanted to go back into the shop | It's rude, and the shopkeeper is cross
exercise: What is une tuque in Quebec? | A woolly hat | A thick scarf | A warm coat | A pair of boots
exercise: In Quebec, Mia hears "Le dîner est prêt !" at noon. Which meal is it? | Lunch | Breakfast | Supper | A snack
exercise: Which two languages does Amira's penpal in Dakar speak? | French and Wolof | French and English | French and Arabic | French and Spanish
exercise: A boy in Abidjan writes "On dit quoi ?" What does he mean? | What's up? | What did you say? | What is your name? | What time is it?
exercise: Which of these is part of France? | La Réunion, in the Indian Ocean | Jersey, in the Channel Islands | Quebec, in Canada | Dakar, in Senegal
exercise: Ollie sees "Rue de..." on a street sign in Jersey. What is a rue? | A street | A river | A shop | A square
exercise: Where is New Caledonia? | In the Pacific, not far from Australia | In the Atlantic, near Canada | In the Indian Ocean, near Madagascar | In the Caribbean, near Martinique
exercise: How is Paris said in French? | "pa-REE" | "PARR-iss" | "pa-RISS" | "PAH-ree"
exercise: Which river runs through Paris? | la Seine | la Loire | le Rhône | la Tamise
exercise: Why was the Eiffel Tower built? | For a world fair in 1889 | For a king's palace in 1689 | For the Olympic Games in 1924 | For a radio station in 1989
exercise: ★★★ A Belgian child works out nonante-neuf moins septante. What is the answer in French? | vingt-neuf | trente-neuf | dix-neuf | vingt-sept
test: Oliver from Leeds says, "French? That's just France, Miss, and maybe a bit of Canada." What does the map show? | French is spoken on every continent | He's right, just France and Canada | French is spoken only in Europe | Only France and Belgium speak French
test: Lily in Brussels hears "septante" and goes, "Belgium's French is broken, innit?" What's the fairest answer? | It's a Belgian way of saying 70 | Yes, Belgians make maths mistakes | It's a Dutch word, not French | It's old French nobody uses
test: ★ Ethan from Boston's aunt in Quebec shouts "Mets ta tuque !" He goes, "A tuck? Like, tuck my shirt in?" What should he do? | Put his woolly hat on | Tuck his shirt in | Eat his snack | Take his shoes off
test: Mia from Ohio is invited for "le dîner" by a family in Quebec and turns up at 7 in the evening. What went wrong? | In Quebec le dîner is lunch, at noon | Nothing, le dîner is always at 7 | In Quebec le dîner is breakfast | In Quebec they never eat dinner
test: Amira from Leicester says, "Two languages at home? That's peng!" (peng = great) Which is true in Senegal? | Many speak French and Wolof | Everyone speaks only Wolof | Everyone speaks only French | People speak English and French
test: ★ Jack from Glasgow answers "On dit quoi ?" with a whole paragraph about his day. What's a better reply? | Ça va un peu, et toi ? | Je m'appelle Jack | Au revoir, à demain | Il est quatre heures
test: Ruby from Cork goes, "Sure Martinique can't be France, it's miles away in the sea!" What is true? | It's part of France | It is part of Spain, in Europe | It is part of England, in the sea | It is its own country, using dollars
test: ★ Ollie from Hull sees French street signs in Jersey and asks, "Are we in France now, like?" What's the answer? | No, Jersey isn't France; it has French names | Yes, Jersey is part of France | Yes, the signs show it's French | No, Jersey is part of Spain
test: ★★★ A Belgian kid says septante-sept and a Paris kid says soixante-dix-sept. Do they mean the same number, and what is it? | Yes, 77 | No, 67 and 77 | Yes, 87 | No, 77 and 97
test: Noah from Liverpool says "PARR-iss" and his teacher says, "Right city, wrong sound." What should he say? | "pa-REE", with a silent s | "pa-RISS", with a soft s | "PAH-reez", with a z | "par-EESS", with an s
test: What would happen if you said "Bonjour !" to leave a shop in Paris? | It'd sound odd: in France say au revoir | It would be perfectly normal everywhere in France | It would be rude enough to get you thrown out | People would think you were from Belgium
test: Ava from Wellington says, "French people near us? Sweet as!" Where could her penpal live? | Nouméa, New Caledonia | Nice, in the south of France | Dakar, in Senegal | Montreal, in Quebec
exam: Identify the continent upon which the greatest number of French speakers reside. | Africa | Europe | North America | Asia
exam: Select the expression employed by French-speaking Belgians to denote the number ninety. | nonante | quatre-vingt-dix | neuvante | huitante
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the status of La Réunion? | It forms part of France, in the Indian Ocean | It is an independent nation in the Pacific | It forms part of Belgium | It is a province of Canada
exam: Determine the meaning of the term "tuque" as employed in Quebec. | A woolly hat | A corner shop | A car | A midday meal
